sasha: heads-up: we got a bug report — types from the published package collapse generics to `any` when imported in an edge runtime. Locally tsc output was fine. anyone seen a similar packaging/tsc mismatch?

liam: yeah. usually it's a post-processing step that rewrites .d.ts. do you run rollup-plugin-dts or any concat step after tsc?

sasha: our pipeline: tsc -> rollup (bundle) -> plugin-dts to flatten types. locally the emitted d.ts has the generic signature, but the final dist/index.d.ts in the tarball only contains the implementation signature (no generic).

zara: can you paste a micro repro? the pattern that often breaks is overloads that differ only by return generic.

sasha: tiny repro below:
```ts
// src/fetcher.ts
export function fetcher<T>(path: string): Promise<T>;
export async function fetcher(path: string) { return fetch(path).then(r => r.json()) }

// consumer.ts
const user = await fetcher<{name:string}>("/user");
// when installed from npm user becomes any
```

liam: yep that matches. plugin-dts and some flatteners will drop overloads or collapse to implementation signature. short fixes: 1) ship tsc-produced .d.ts unchanged, 2) avoid ambiguous overloads and use single generic signature like `export function fetcher<T = unknown>(path: string): Promise<T>`

sasha: i tried the single-signature hack and it fixed the consumer typing in edge. but i want a proper pipeline fix.

zara: also inspect package.json: ensure `types` points to the correct file emitted by tsc and that `exports` entries don't accidentally point to a different .d.ts for the import path used by ESM consumers. TS resolves types differently for `import` vs `require`.

ci-bot: [pack-verify #701] failed: mismatch between tsc ./dist/index.d.ts and packaged ./index.d.ts. logs: https://ci.redwood/pack-verify/701/logs

rui: long-term we should add a CI job that: runs `npm pack`, installs the packed tarball into a tiny fixture project (NodeNext + classic), and runs `tsc --noEmit` to detect declaration regressions. also add `declarationMap: true` during builds to make root-cause tracing easier.

liam: +1. i'd also add a lint rule discouraging overloads that only differ by return generic; they're brittle across toolchains. and prefer named exports for libraries expected to be used in mixed ESM/CJS/edge environments.

sasha: thanks all. i'll: 1) revert the dts flatten step for SDK packages, 2) add the npm-pack tsc smoke test in CI, and 3) sweep a few hotspots to replace overloads with single generic signatures. leaving a note here in case anyone else hits generics -> `any` after publishing. :memo:
